 CellChangeTimes->{{3.9792992443987007`*^9, 3.979299296310844*^9}, 
   3.9793004566640053`*^9, {3.9794204272813187`*^9, 3.979420427696233*^9}, 
   3.979427542677999*^9},ExpressionUUID->"e8c084b9-dff9-475e-b4db-\
b27287a31c5d"],

Cell["\<\
In this bookmarked visualization, a particle traces a donut-shaped shell \
around the black hole. This is the Lense-Thirring Effect at moderate setting. \
The phenomenon changing the osculating plane is nodal precession.\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.9793009488319063`*^9, 3.979301014262206*^9}, {
  3.979427554215528*^9, 
  3.979427585451243*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"47417b78-0925-4ca0-bcbc-\
54e15ae60ccb"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["The Setup",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 ": The black hole is spinning rapidly (",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"a", "=", "0.9"}], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "781c8f10-652a-4d33-8a12-70f335751c54"],
 "). The particle has small slight vertical kick ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"(", 
    RowBox[{
     SubscriptBox["v", "\[Theta]"], "=", "0.0052"}], ")"}], TraditionalForm]],
  ExpressionUUID->"3924e478-5b70-47a2-a368-f8ac1f5419f1"],
 " out of the equatorial plane."
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.9793009488319063`*^9, 3.979301102836081*^9}, {
  3.979301177707121*^9, 3.9793011899921083`*^9}, {3.979427592137315*^9, 
  3.979427603070324*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"d29dc8b0-7182-413d-8eee-\
bd6f212f3424"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["The Result",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 ": With a non-rotating Schwarzschild black hole, this orbit would just be a \
precessing ellipse that stays on a single flat plane. But here, the black \
hole\[CloseCurlyQuote]s spin drags the entire space around with it, adding \
nodal precession."
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.9793009488319063`*^9, 3.979301102836081*^9}, {
  3.979301177707121*^9, 3.979301274135968*^9}, {3.979427612239108*^9, 
  3.9794276263499537`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"a77db42a-b3f8-43f6-a836-\
23c9216e46fe"],

Cell["\<\
As the particle orbits, the osculating plane (instantaneous plane of travel) \
is continuously twisted by the black hole\[CloseCurlyQuote]s spin. The nodes, \
where the orbit crosses the equator, circulate over time, causing the orbit \
to precess not just in the radial direction, like Mercury\[CloseCurlyQuote]s \
perihelion precession around the Sun, but in azimuth, painting a complex \
torus (donut) shape instead of just a  ring. Near a spinning black hole, \
there is no such thing as a fixed orbital plane.\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.9793009488319063`*^9, 3.979301102836081*^9}, {
  3.979301177707121*^9, 3.97930140298267*^9}, {3.9794276387506933`*^9, 
  3.979427658867649*^9}, {3.9794276893104763`*^9, 
  3.979427697033692*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"a5048520-864c-419d-9894-\
fa7d3632353e"]
}, Open  ]],

